ted into the work  everything everything  that is made to be consumed including  art because i don't care how artsy it is  it's still intended to be viewed by  somebody has a political aspect and if  you are not prepared to accept that i  honestly don't know how to have this  conversation with you  but assuming that you can go with me  that far  accepting that there is a political  element to everything  let's talk specifically about the  politics of inclusion  and why or at least my perspective  on why because i'm not an expert  the it is such a sticking point  especially in internet discourse and  geek internet discourse  in particular much to my chagrin  well the first thing that really needs  to be addressed  is in some ways the language i run into  this every now and then that sometimes  part of the problem is the way that we  talk about things  and just the fact that the term  political correctness exists  is part of why people when they see  inclusion shake a finger and go politics  because we actually have the word  political right in the thing that  we use as an umbrella that these that  this sort of thing falls under  now political correctness was a term  originally coined in the 70s and  originally it was much more about the  specifics about  how someone was portrayed as opposed to  their mere  inclusion and presence at all but at  this point especially in terms of  popular use it absolutely all falls  under that same umbrella but i kind of  want  to shove that term aside  forget the fact that the word politics  exists in this  term that is not what makes it political  because that's just the language just  that's just what we use to describe it  that does not inherently mean that that  word  is the best thing that we should be  doing or is indicative of exactly what  it is we're dealing with  but as i said at the front end inclusion  is political acknowledging the existence  of a group especially a minority or  underprivileged or underrepresented  group 